# Copy this file to .env and fill in the token. .env is gitignored — keep it that way.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Reading the digest needs no token at all; the repo is public. You only need one to
# push an updated digest.html / manifest.json. See skills/common/references/github-sync.md
# for the write procedure.
#
# Minting the token — GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens
# → Fine-grained tokens → Generate new token:
#
#   Repository access ...... Only select repositories → ai-news-research-skill
#   Permissions ............ Repository permissions → Contents: Read and write
#                            (nothing else — no Metadata beyond the read it forces,
#                             no Actions, no Workflows)
#   Expiration ............. whatever you'll tolerate re-minting. 90 days is fine;
#                            this token does one job and losing it costs one page visit.
#
# A classic token with `repo` scope also works, but it can write every repo you own.
# Prefer the fine-grained one — the whole point is that a leak costs you one public
# repo of AI news summaries.
#
# Using it locally:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a      # loads GH_TOKEN without putting it in argv or history
#   python3 push.py digest.html digest.html "digest: ... (claude, rev N)"
#   unset GH_TOKEN
#
# Using it in a web chat (Claude/Grok, no clone): upload this file — the filled-in .env,
# not the example — when the agent asks, at the moment it's pushing. Understand that it
# then lives in that conversation, exactly as a pasted token would. The scope and expiry
# above are what make that acceptable.
#
# If it ever leaks: revoke at GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access
# tokens → the token → Delete. Then mint a new one; nothing here caches it.

GH_TOKEN=
